or  the electric trigger which will which  will again reduce false positives but  also has the risk of making your alert  be less likely to fire based on the  shape of your data and we'll have a  demonstration later that illustrates  that point so now let's go ahead and  save our condition will also add a  notification channel so that when our  alert fires we can be notified on email  slack pager duty etc so we'll go ahead  and say that we want an email to be sent  to you food example.com and lastly we'll  put in some documentation that explains  to the person on-call how they might  attempt to remediate the issue caused by  the alert so we might say that they  should check the database locks they  should check the amount of allocatable  CPU quota for the pods etc now we'll go  ahead and save this policy now we see a  graph here again of our time series we  also see incidents that will this is  where once the alert starts firing  incidents will appear now we can go  ahead to see what that looks like we can  go into our list of existing incidents  we'll select one of them and here we see  in the message field it tells us exactly  what resource this particular gke node  caused the alert to trigger based on a  certain time series value over our  threshold we can see the graph here of  all of our nodes so that we can easily  compare the node in violation to the  performance of the other nodes and we  see some labels here that help us again  zero down and two and figure out what  the exact resources that's causing our  issue lastly we can also link out  directly to logs and other ops  management tools that help us figure out  what the root cause of the problem is  and to remediate the issue for a second  demo we're going to talk about absence  alerts and how they differ slightly from  threshold based alerts to illustrate the  concepts behind absence alerts let's go  ahead and  select a log based metric that we  created from one of our App Engine apps  and in this example the App Engine app  was spun up at this point in time it  started to write logs and then we  disabled the app at which point it  stopped writing logs as you can see here  this graph is expressed as a rate which  is why it's spiky it's how frequently  the log is being written in our case for  an absence alert we we really don't care  about the rate we want to just see the  count or the number of time series  points that were written for the log  based metric so in this case we will  apply a count aligner which gives us a  nice smooth line here again we have kind  of a step function we see missing data  or absent data here here is metric data  and then it goes absent again right here  in order to create an absence alert for  this metric what you want to do is focus  on the bottom you want to say instead of  condition is above you say is absent  then the threshold will go away and  underneath the four you have the amount  of minutes that you actually worry about  this metric being absent before your  phone goes off or before you get another  type of communication in this case we'll  choose 10 minutes and the last thing  here is to clicking again on show  Advanced Options you'll note that if you  want to a granade so for example you  don't care that this log space metric is  absent for any specific custom label but  you care that the metric doesn't exist  at all you may want to pick an  aggregator when that happens you will  need to pick an aligner like count for  alignment period we suggest just picking  one minute that way you don't have to  think about it you can focus on the  important parameter which is the amount  of time that the metric is absent in our  third demo we'll discuss how alignment  period in duration interact with each  other and how it affects whether or not  you are  Erbs fire reliable to further illustrate  the differences between alignment period  in duration let's look at an example  here where we're starting to see a real  spiky increase in network traffic we've  selected the received bytes metric for  our GCE vm instance we see over here are  received by count which is spiking up  and down over time we've also filtered  this down to the traffic sent to a very  specific VM the demo VM and so we've  gone down here and said we want to be  alerted if the time series values are  above this threshold for an amount of  time let's say we want to be alerted if  it's above the threshold for 10 minutes  now  naively this might make sense but will  this alert fire every time the line here  on the graph crosses the threshold and  the answer is No based on the  demonstration earlier we know that we  care about the 10 minute summary of  bytes received to do that we should not  change this field in the 4 field instead  we should go to Advanced Options pick an  alignment period of 10 minutes and then  pick a statistic that we like in this  case rate over 10 minutes sounds  reasonable this is the average bytes  received per second over every 10 minute  window you can see that the curve is  smoother better representing the fact  that you care about a 10 minute average  after this for the 4 field which was it  10 minutes the best thing to do is to  set this two most recent value that way  every time this smooth line goes above  the threshold your alert will fire and  your alert will not flap because  remember you have a smooth curve it's  going to take some effort for that curve  to come back down so now that we have  correctly set the alignment period and  duration values let's go ahead and save  this condition and one other thing that  we want to illustrate is the ability to  add multiple conditions to an alert  policy when we want to detect the ping  storm is happening we're interested not  only in the receive  by its for one VM for our demo VM but  we're actually interested in the  behavior of another VM so let's go ahead  and look at our GCE instances and in  this case we're interested in CPU  utilization for a different VM this one  by the name of instance one so now we  see that now that we've filtered down to  this particular instance we see here  that instance one CPU utilization has  been pegged at 100% for quite some time  now so we might want to say that if our  CPU utilization is over 90% for a 10  minute alignment period then alert us  when the most recent value crosses the  threshold and we'll go ahead and click  Add there you see now that we've added  two conditions we have another option  that the policy will trigger when it  defaults to any condition is met so if  either of these conditions trigger  you'll receive an alert you also have  the option to say if all conditions are  met so both would have to both  conditions would have to be true and  then lastly in our case we will select  all conditions are met even for  different resources free for each  condition so if you remember in our case  the first condition was filtered down to  the instance ID demo and the second  condition was filtered or predicated on  the instance ID instance 1 so this has  been an example that not only  illustrates both the interrelationship  between alignment and duration but also  how you would combine multiple  conditions within your alert policy and  that is it for our intro to alerting and  cloud monitoring thanks for listening  and have a nice day
 

